Step 1: Navigate to your Exercise 7 directory
cd ~/Projects/devopsexp/Dokerdemo

Check that app.py and Dockerfile contain the web application version, not the simple print("Hello") version.

Step 2: Build the Docker image
docker build -t python-demo:1.0 .

Load the image into Minikube:

minikube image load python-demo:1.0
Step 3: Create a Kubernetes configuration file
nano k8s-app.yaml

Paste this configuration:


apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: python-web
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: python-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: python-web
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: python-web
          image: python-demo:1.0
          imagePullPolicy: Never
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8000
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: python-web-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: python-web
  ports:
    - port: 8000
      targetPort: 8000

Save using Ctrl+O, Enter, and Ctrl+X.

What this does:

replicas: 2 asks Kubernetes to maintain two copies of your web application.

containerPort: 8000 identifies the port your Python server listens on.

The Service provides a common endpoint for accessing the application.

imagePullPolicy: Never tells Kubernetes to use the locally loaded image.

Step 4: Deploy the application

Make sure Minikube is running:

minikube start --driver=docker

Apply the configuration:

kubectl apply -f k8s-app.yaml
Step 5: Verify automatic deployment
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods
kubectl get services

Wait until both pods are Running and the deployment shows 2/2 ready replicas.

Step 6: Access the web application

Run:

minikube service python-web-service --url

Open the URL returned by the command in your browser. You should see:

Hello from Docker!

My web application is running inside a Docker container.

Keep the terminal running if the command maintains a forwarding process.

Step 7: Demonstrate Kubernetes automation

Scale the application from two replicas to three:

kubectl scale deployment python-web --replicas=3

Verify:

kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods

You should see three ready replicas once Kubernetes finishes creating the additional pod.

Restore the original two-replica configuration:

kubectl apply -f k8s-app.yaml
Step 8: Clean up after the experiment

If you need to remove the Kubernetes resources after your demonstration:

kubectl delete -f k8s-app.yaml

This deletes the Deployment and Service created by this configuration, without removing your Docker image or uninstalling Docker.
